Why Is BCD Technology Important for Power Management?

BCD processes integrate bipolar devices, CMOS logic and DMOS power transistors on one wafer platform. This allows designers to combine control circuits, analog functions and power-handling capabilities within a single chip.

The technology supports power-management ICs, DC-DC converters, battery-management systems, motor drivers, audio power devices and high-voltage interfaces. Foundries compete by offering suitable voltage classes, device libraries, embedded memory, thick-metal options and qualified manufacturing processes.

Automotive Electronics Supports Foundry Demand

Electric vehicles, advanced driver assistance systems, battery management, electronic control units and vehicle lighting require increasingly sophisticated power electronics.

Automotive products also involve extended qualification cycles and long manufacturing lifetimes. These characteristics make stable process availability, reliability and long-term foundry support important considerations for chip designers.

AI Data Centers Create Additional Power-Management Opportunities

AI accelerators and high-performance processors require efficient conversion of electrical power across multiple voltage levels. BCD technologies can support selected power-management devices used in server, cloud and telecommunications infrastructure.

The best process node depends on voltage requirements, integration density, power losses, reliability and cost. Smaller geometries are not automatically optimal for every high-voltage or analog application.

Wafer Size and Process Nodes Shape the Market

The report identifies 12-inch, 8-inch and 6-inch BCD platforms. Larger wafers can improve production economics for suitable high-volume devices, while established 200 mm and 150 mm lines remain relevant for mature, specialized and long-lifecycle products.

Technology nodes also vary according to the application. Mature processes can remain attractive for high-voltage and automotive devices, while smaller nodes support higher digital integration and more compact power-management designs.

